    "__s": [
      "What was the first Kirby game?",
      "The first **Kirby** game was **_Kirby's Dream Land_**, released for the **Game Boy** in **1992**.\n\nIf you want, I can also tell you:\n- the first **Kirby game ever released in Japan**\n- the first **Kirby game in the timeline**\n- or the first **Kirby game by console**",
      "The first **Kirby** game was **Kirby’s Dream Land**, released for the **Game Boy** in **1992**.\n\nIt introduced Kirby and the basic series formula: inhaling enemies, floating, and copying abilities came later as the series developed.",
      "The first Kirby game was **Kirby’s Dream Land**, released for the **Game Boy** in **1992**. It introduced Kirby and set up the early series.",
      "Kirby",
      "The first Kirby game is **Kirby's Dream Land**, which was released for the **Game Boy** in **1992**.",
      "The first game in the Kirby series is **Kirby's Dream Land**, released for the Nintendo Game Boy in **1992**.",
      "What was the first Legend of Zelda game?",
      "The first game in the series is simply titled ***The Legend of Zelda***. It was released in Japan for the Famicom Disk System in February 1986, and released in North America for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in August 1987.",
      "The first game in the series is simply titled ***The Legend of Zelda***. It was released for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan in 1986 and in North America in 1987.",
      "Legend of Zelda",
      "The first **The Legend of Zelda** game was **_The Legend of Zelda_**, released in **1986** for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in Japan, and in **1987** in North America.\n\nIt was the start of the long-running Zelda series.",
      "The first **Legend of Zelda** game was **_The Legend of Zelda_**, released in **1986** for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in Japan, and in **1987** in North America.",
      "What was the first Super Mario Bros game?",
      "The first game in the series is simply titled **\"Super Mario Bros.\"**\n\nIt was released in Japan on **September 13, 1985**, for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES/Famicom). \n\nIt is important to note that while this was the first game *titled* \"Super Mario Bros.,\" Mario himself appeared earlier in other arcade games, most notably as \"Jumpman\" in the 1981 game **Donkey Kong** and the 1983 game **Mario Bros.**",
      "The first game in the series is simply titled **\"Super Mario Bros.\"**\n\nIt was released in Japan on **September 13, 1985**, for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) / Famicom. While Mario had appeared in earlier arcade games like *Donkey Kong* (1981) and *Mario Bros.* (1983), the 1985 title was the first to feature the platforming gameplay that defined the series.",
      "The first game in the series is **\"Super Mario Bros.\"**, released in **1985** for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES).\n\nWhile Mario actually made his debut earlier in the 1981 arcade game *Donkey Kong* (where he was known as \"Jumpman\") and the 1983 arcade game *Mario Bros.*, *Super Mario Bros.* (1985) was the first title to feature the side-scrolling platforming gameplay that defined the franchise.",
      "Super Mario Bros",
      "What was the first Pokemon game?",
      "The first Pokémon games ever released were **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Green**, which launched in Japan on the Game Boy on **February 27, 1996**.\n\nA few important details regarding their history:\n\n*   **International Release:** While Japan got Red and Green, the rest of the world received **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Blue** in 1998. The international \"Blue\" version was based on the slightly updated *Pokémon Blue* (released later in Japan) which improved graphics and sound.\n*   **Pokémon Green:** Because of technical issues in the original Japanese *Red* and *Green*, they were later followed by a more polished version called *Pokémon Blue* in Japan. When the series was brought to North America and Europe, Nintendo used the code from the Japanese *Blue* version as the base for the international *Red* and *Blue*.\n*   **The Mascot:** While Red and Green were the first, the phenomenon really exploded with the later release of **Pokémon Yellow**, which was designed to mirror the anime and featured Pikachu as the starter Pokémon.",
      "The first Pokémon games ever released were **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Green**, which launched for the Game Boy in Japan on **February 27, 1996**.\n\nBecause *Pokémon Green* was never released outside of Japan, the international audience’s introduction to the series came in 1998 with **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Blue** (which were based on the updated Japanese version, *Pokémon Blue*).",
      "The first Pokémon games ever released were **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Green**, which launched for the Game Boy in Japan on February 27, 1996.\n\nIn international markets, these were followed by **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Blue** (using the game engine from the Japanese version of *Pokémon Blue*), which were released in North America in 1998 and Europe in 1999.",
      "Pokemon",
      "The first Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Green**, released in **Japan in 1996**.\n\nIf you mean the **first Pokémon game released outside Japan**, that was **Pokémon Red and Pokémon Blue**.",
      "The first Pokémon game released was **Pokémon Red and Green** in **Japan** for the **Game Boy** in **1996**.\n\nIf you mean the first Pokémon game released internationally, it was **Pokémon Red and Blue**.",
      "The first Pokémon game released was **Pokémon Red and Green** in Japan, in **1996**.\n\nIf you mean the first Pokémon game released internationally, it was **Pokémon Red and Blue**.",
      "The first Legend of Zelda game was **The Legend of Zelda**, released in 1986 for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). It was developed and published by Nintendo and became one of the most influential video games of all time, establishing many of the series' core elements like puzzles, exploration, and the iconic protagonist Link.",
      "The first Legend of Zelda game was **The Legend of Zelda**, released in 1986 for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). It was developed and published by Nintendo and is considered a landmark title that helped define the action-adventure genre. The game was a critical and commercial success and spawned one of Nintendo's most iconic and long-running franchises.",
      "The first Legend of Zelda game was **The Legend of Zelda**, released in 1986 for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). It was developed and published by Nintendo and is considered a landmark title in video game history, helping to establish many conventions of the action-adventure genre.",
      "The first Pokémon game was **Pokémon Red and Green**, released in Japan for the Game Boy on **February 27, 1996**. These two versions were followed by *Pokémon Blue* (a revised version) later that year. Internationally, *Pokémon Red* and *Blue* were released in 1998 as the first games in the series.",
      "The first Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red and Green**, released in Japan on **February 27, 1996** for the Game Boy. However, internationally (including North America), the first releases were **Pokémon Red and Blue**, which came out in 1998 and were based on an updated version of the Japanese games. Pokémon Green was never released outside of Japan in its original form.",
      "The first Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red** and **Pokémon Green**, released in Japan for the Game Boy on **February 27, 1996**. (Internationally, Pokémon Red and Blue were released in 1998.)",
      "The first Kirby game was **Kirby's Dream Land**, released on April 27, 1992, for the Nintendo Game Boy. \n\nIn this game, players control Kirby as he travels through Dream Land to defeat King Dedede and recover the stolen Sparkling Stars. It introduced Kirby's signature move of inhaling enemies and either spitting them out or swallowing them to copy their abilities. The game was a commercial and critical success, launching one of Nintendo's most beloved franchises.",
      "The first Kirby game was **Kirby's Dream Land**, released in Japan on April 27, 1992, for the Game Boy. It came out in North America later that same year.\n\nIn this game, players control Kirby as he travels through Dream Land to defeat King Dedede. A notable feature of the original game was that Kirby couldn't actually copy abilities yet—that signature mechanic was introduced in later games. Kirby's Dream Land was a commercial and critical success, launching one of Nintendo's most beloved and enduring franchises.",
      "The first Kirby game was **Kirby's Dream Land**, released for the Game Boy on April 27, 1992 in Japan and later that year in North America. \n\nIn this game, players control Kirby as he travels through Dream Land to stop King Dedede from stealing the Sparkling Stars. The game introduced Kirby's signature copy ability, where he can inhale enemies and gain their powers. It was a major success and launched one of Nintendo's most beloved franchises.",
      "The first **Super Mario Bros.** game was **Super Mario Bros.**, released for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in **1985** in Japan and **1986** in North America.\n\nIf you mean the first game featuring Mario at all, that was **Donkey Kong** in **1981**, where he appeared as “Jumpman.”",
      "The first **Super Mario Bros.** game was **_Super Mario Bros._**, released for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in **1985**.\n\nIf you meant the very first Mario game overall, that was **_Mario Bros._** in **1983**.",
      "The first **Super Mario Bros.** game was **_Super Mario Bros._**, released for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in **1985**.\n\nIf you mean the first **Mario** game overall, that was **_Mario Bros._** in **1983**.",
      "The first Pokémon game was **Pokémon Red and Green** (released in Japan on February 27, 1996 for the Game Boy).\n\nIn North America and Europe, the first widely available Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red and Blue**, which were released in September 1998 and October 1999, respectively.\n\nThese games introduced the world to the Pokémon franchise and featured 151 Pokémon (including Mew), with the goal of catching and training creatures to battle other trainers.",
      "The first Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red and Green**, released in Japan on February 27, 1996 for the Game Boy.\n\nThese were later followed by **Pokémon Blue** (also in Japan), and then **Pokémon Red and Blue** were released internationally in North America in 1998.\n\nThe games featured 151 Pokémon (including the secret Mew) and introduced the core gameplay of catching, training, and battling creatures.",
      "The first Pokémon games were **Pokémon Red and Green**, released in Japan on February 27, 1996, for the Game Boy.\n\nThese were later released internationally as **Pokémon Red and Blue** in 1998. The games introduced the original 151 Pokémon (though technically 150, since Mew was secret) and featured the Kanto region.\n\nA enhanced version called **Pokémon Yellow** was also released later, which followed the storyline of the anime series more closely.",
      "The first Super Mario Bros game was released on September 13, 1985, for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan, and October 1, 1985, in North America.\n\nIt was developed and published by Nintendo and became one of the most iconic and influential video games of all time. The game featured Mario (originally called \"Jumpman\") as he attempted to rescue Princess Toadstool from Bowser's castle, jumping over obstacles and collecting power-ups along the way. It's widely credited with revitalizing the video game industry after the 1983 crash and establishing many conventions of the platformer genre that are still used today.",
      "The first Super Mario Bros game was released on September 13, 1985, for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan, and October 18, 1985, in North America.\n\nIt was developed by Nintendo and is considered one of the most influential and iconic video games of all time. The game featured Mario (originally called \"Jumpman\") as he tried to rescue Princess Peach from Bowser in the Mushroom Kingdom. It became a massive success and helped revitalize the video game industry after the 1983 crash, while also establishing many of the gameplay elements and characters that would define the Mario franchise for decades to come.",
      "The first Super Mario Bros game was released on September 13, 1985, for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). It was developed and published by Nintendo and became one of the most iconic and influential video games of all time. The game featured Mario, a plumber, who had to rescue Princess Peach from the villain Bowser across eight worlds. It was a massive commercial success and played a crucial role in revitalizing the video game industry after the crash of 1983.",
      "The first *Legend of Zelda* game was simply titled **\"The Legend of Zelda\"** (often referred to as *The Legend of Zelda 1* or the *Original Legend of Zelda*). It was developed and published by Nintendo for the **Famicom Disk System** in Japan on **February 21, 1986**, and later released for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)** in North America in **1987**. It introduced the iconic hero Link, Princess Zelda, and the villain Ganon.",
      "The first *Legend of Zelda* game is simply titled **The Legend of Zelda** (often referred to as *The Legend of Zelda* or *Zelda I*). \n\nIt was released in **1986** for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan (in 1987 in North America). It introduced players to the land of Hyrule, the hero Link, Princess Zelda, and the Triforce, and it was revolutionary for its open-world, non-linear gameplay.",
      "The first Legend of Zelda game is **\"The Legend of Zelda\"**, originally released in **1986** in Japan (and 1987 in North America) for the **Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)**. It was created by Shigeru Miyamoto and Takashi Tezuka, and it established the series' core gameplay of exploration, puzzle-solving, and dungeon crawling.",
      "The first Kirby game was **\"Kirby's Dream Land,\"** released for the **Nintendo Game Boy** in **1992** in Japan and North America. \n\nIt was a simple, side-scrolling platformer and was notably different from later games in the series—Kirby couldn't copy abilities yet; he could only inhale enemies and spit them out. It was also very short and easy, designed by Masahiro Sakurai to be accessible to beginners. \n\n**Fun fact:** Kirby's original name in Japan was **\"Titirino\"** (ティティリノ) as a placeholder, and his name was changed to Kirby for the western release.",
      " The first Kirby game was **Kirby's Dream Land**, released for the Nintendo Game Boy on **April 27, 1992** in Japan (and later in North America in August 1992).\n\nIt was a simple, short platformer where Kirby (then known as \"Popopo\" in early promotional material) had to retrieve the stolen food from King Dedede. Interestingly, in this debut title, Kirby didn't have his signature copy ability—that wasn't introduced until the next game, **Kirby's Adventure** (1993) for the NES.",
      "The first Kirby game was **\"Kirby's Dream Land\"**, released for the **Nintendo Game Boy** in **1992** (in Japan and North America). It was not the first game in the series chronologically (that would be *Kirby's Dream Land*), but it was the first to feature the character Kirby. The game was notable for introducing Kirby's signature inhale-and-copy ability, which only appeared in later titles, as the original game (Kirby's Dream Land) did not have the copy ability.",
      "The first Super Mario Bros. game was simply titled **Super Mario Bros.**, released for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan on September 13, 1985, and in North America later that year. \n\nHowever, it's worth noting that Mario actually made his first appearance in the arcade game *Donkey Kong* in 1981, and he starred in the arcade game *Mario Bros.* in 1983. But *Super Mario Bros.* was the first game in the mainline *Super Mario* series and the one that introduced the iconic side-scrolling platforming gameplay, power-ups like the Super Mushroom and Fire Flower, and the setting of the Mushroom Kingdom.",
      "The first game in the Super Mario Bros. series was simply titled **\"Super Mario Bros.\"** It was released for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES) in Japan on **September 13, 1985**, and later in North America later that year.\n\nIt was developed and published by Nintendo, and it marked the first official side-scrolling platforming adventure for Mario (previously known as \"Jumpman\" from the arcade game *Donkey Kong*).",
      " The first game in the *Super Mario Bros.* series was simply titled ***Super Mario Bros.***  \n\nIt was released in **1985** for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). It is the game that introduced the world to Mario (and Luigi) in their quest to rescue Princess Toadstool from the villain Bowser, and it essentially saved the home video game industry and established the platformer genre as we know it today."
